{
  "title": "ULEARN \u2014 edtech (689\u2605)",
  "content": "Project: ULEARN\nDomain: edtech | Scale: small | Stars: 689\nProblem: ULEARN provides a free, open-source Learning Management System (LMS) that enables educators to create and manage online courses with multi-role support for admins, instructors, and students.\nTarget users: online educators and instructors, students, e-learning platform administrators, educational institutions\nCore features: Course creation and management with multimedia curriculum (audio, video, document, text); Multi-role access control (Admin, Instructor, Student); Payment gateway integration for course purchases; Social login authentication; Instructor credits and earnings module; Blog and page management (CMS); SEO-friendly responsive frontend website; Interactive ReactJS-powered course learning page\nIntegrations: social login providers (OAuth), payment gateway (unspecified)\nTech stack: ReactJS 16.9 with React Router, Bootstrap 4 + Laravel 5.8 (PHP) + MySQL (via Laravel Eloquent/Artisan migrations) + Laravel Mix (Webpack) for asset bundling\nKey libraries: react, react-dom, react-router-dom, axios, laravel-mix, bootstrap, jquery, @fortawesome/react-fontawesome, sass\nArchitecture: monolith\nDesign lessons:\n- Hybrid rendering approach: Laravel handles server-side routing and blade templates while ReactJS is used selectively for interactive course-learning pages rather than as a full SPA.\n- Laravel Mix is used as the build pipeline abstraction over Webpack, simplifying asset compilation for a PHP-centric team.\n- Multi-role RBAC (Admin, Instructor, Student) is baked into the core design, reflecting the hierarchical nature of LMS platforms.\n- The project relies on Laravel's Artisan migrations and seeders for schema management and initial data population, enabling quick local setup.",
